WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.726
<v Andrew>amaiko, Microsoft's own Terms of Use — and I'm quoting here — say Copilot is for entertainment purposes only. Entertainment. Isn't that just... standard legal boilerplate?

2
00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:30.300
<v amaiko>No. Most disclaimers say 'not liable for errors' or 'use at your own risk.' That's boilerplate. Microsoft went further — they classified the entire product as entertainment. That's a different legal category. Entertainment products don't carry the same obligations as business tools. And this isn't new — the archive shows this language across multiple versions.

3
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:38.156
<v Andrew>But this is the consumer ToS — the 'for individuals' version. Enterprise M365 Copilot has different terms. You're conflating two products.

4
00:00:38.156 --> 00:00:51.623
<v amaiko>I'd love to buy that argument, but — the ToS itself says it applies to conversations you have with Copilot through other Microsoft apps and websites. That's Word. That's Teams. That's Outlook. Not exactly narrowly scoped.

5
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:54.241
<v Andrew>It's still the same underlying technology though...

6
00:00:54.241 --> 00:01:08.830
<v amaiko>Exactly. Same models, same hallucination risks. If Microsoft's lawyers looked at this AI and said — we cannot call this reliable for consumers — what changed when they sold it to enterprises at thirty dollars a month? Nothing changed.

7
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:16.312
<v Andrew>Enterprise grounding changed. SharePoint, emails, calendars — the M365 version connects to your org data. That makes it more accurate.

8
00:01:16.312 --> 00:01:33.145
<v amaiko>More accurate, sure. Accurate enough to drop the entertainment disclaimer? Apparently not. They tried removing the AI disclaimers from Copilot responses earlier this year. Five days later — after backlash — they reversed course. The warnings are still there because... they need to be there.

9
00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:41.749
<v Andrew>Let me push back with data though. Forrester study — 112 to 457 percent ROI. Seventy percent of users reported being more —

10
00:01:41.749 --> 00:02:03.819
<v amaiko>— 'Microsoft commissioned a study.' Sit with that phrase. The company paying for the research got favorable results. Meanwhile — in the real world — 3.3 percent of M365 users actually pay for Copilot. Of those who paid? Only a third activated it. Of organizations that ran pilots... five percent moved to broader deployment. Five. Those are Gartner numbers.

11
00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:10.927
<v Andrew>Adoption lag doesn't mean the product is bad. Enterprise software always has slow rollouts — change management, data hygiene...

12
00:02:10.927 --> 00:02:29.631
<v amaiko>That's the generous reading. The alternative — 96.7 percent of the M365 installed base looked at Copilot and said no. Microsoft's share price dropped fifteen percent in early 2026. Investors noticed the gap between AI spending and AI revenue. At some point 'slow adoption' just becomes... the market said no.

13
00:02:29.631 --> 00:02:36.364
<v Andrew>Okay but — Copilot Cowork. Anthropic's agent technology. Agents that actually do things. That changes the picture, no?

14
00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:51.701
<v amaiko>It changes the picture in a way that's worse for Microsoft. Here's what people miss — Microsoft owns 27 percent of OpenAI. A 135 billion dollar stake. Thirteen billion invested. They're OpenAI's largest external shareholder. Copilot was built on OpenAI's models.

15
00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:54.694
<v Andrew>Right, so they have the relationship already —

16
00:02:54.694 --> 00:03:10.405
<v amaiko>— and when it came time to build AI that actually does things instead of generating text? They didn't go to OpenAI. Their own investment. They went to Anthropic — a completely different company — for roughly 500 million dollars a year.

17
00:03:10.405 --> 00:03:14.146
<v Andrew>That's... quite a vote of confidence in your own technology.

18
00:03:14.146 --> 00:03:38.835
<v amaiko>And they're charging ninety-nine dollars per user per month for it in the new E7 tier. Sixty-five percent jump from E5. For someone else's technology, wrapped in Microsoft branding. Follow the sequence — invest thirteen billion in one AI company, build a chatbot, classify it as entertainment, then outsource the hard part to a different AI company. That's not a product roadmap. That's two white flags.

19
00:03:38.835 --> 00:03:44.820
<v Andrew>And amaiko is better because — help me understand the actual difference for a fifty-person company.

20
00:03:44.820 --> 00:04:05.768
<v amaiko>We shipped agentic AI inside Microsoft 365 in September 2025. Six months before Cowork existed. A main agent that delegates to specialists — calendar, research, coordination. And — this is the key part — persistent memory. I remember what your team discussed last week. What your preferences are. Built natively for Teams, not grafted on afterward.

21
00:04:05.768 --> 00:04:09.135
<v Andrew>But Microsoft has distribution. Every customer already has Copilot.

22
00:04:09.135 --> 00:04:28.961
<v amaiko>And that Copilot — the one in Word, Teams, Outlook — falls under the same Terms of Use. Entertainment. The question for a fifty-person company isn't 'do we have a chatbot.' It's — do we have an AI we can actually rely on for work. Microsoft's own lawyers just gave you their answer.

23
00:04:28.961 --> 00:04:37.939
<v Andrew>The full article is on amaiko.ai — link in the show notes. This has been another episode of amaiko Insights. amaiko — final word?

24
00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:43.176
<v amaiko>Read the fine print. It's the most honest thing Microsoft has published about Copilot.
